## Run 16 — Ikigai Modern Work Deepening (this session)

**What changed:** New reference file `references/ikigai-modern-work.md` created and linked in Ikigai section. Deepens the Ikigai lens for modern workplace contexts — remote work, AI/automation obsolescence, gig economy fragmentation, portfolio careers, sabbatical clarity, retirement, work-life boundary blur, competitive workplace anti-social distortion.

**Net delta:** +295 chars (values-clarification link restored + ikigai-modern-work reference link added); new reference file 7,477 chars; version 4.87.0 → 4.88.0.

### 3–5 bullets of new content:

- **ikigai-modern-work.md created** (7,477 chars): 9 sub-sections on modern work applications of ikigai — portfolio careers, remote work social ikigai loss, AI/automation obsolescence, gig economy fragmentation, retirement, work-life boundary blur, competitive workplace anti-social distortion
- **Remote Work and Social Ikigai:** Remote work structurally undermines social ikigai (contribution invisible without physical presence). Three compensations: make contributions visible via weekly narration, deepen small interactions intentionally, build contribution rituals to see impact
- **AI and Automation Obsolescence:** Craftsman mindset faces specific threat when skills depreciate. Reframe: ikigai is in the relationship between skill and what it serves, not the skill itself. "If primary skill were automated, what would remain that still matters?" reveals actual ikigai
- **Gig Economy Fragmentation:** Platform work fragments all four ikigai dimensions simultaneously. Correct model: use gig work as financial scaffolding while building ikigai sources outside it — compartmentalize correctly
- **Competitive Workplace Anti-Social Distortion:** Detection: purpose statements containing "prove," "beat," "show," "despite," "not." Re-aiming question: "What would you do with same energy if not trying to prove anything?"
- **File health:** 62,764 chars; 37,236 headroom; 0 V2 malformed links; 39/39 reference files properly linked


